// Each entry is an array of paragraph translations aligned 1:1 with the
// paragraphs of the passage's `text` (split on blank lines). The reading
// view interleaves these below each Thai paragraph when translation is on.

export const PASSAGE_TRANSLATIONS = {
  "ผ่อนบ้าน 10 ปี — Paying Off a Home Loan": [
    `One asset we're all sure to consider buying at some point in life is a "house." But given prices that are, frankly, anything but cheap, if we waited to save up cash to buy one, it could easily take many years — maybe even a decade. That's why using a bank's home loan service has become such a popular choice.`,
    `Generally, when you take out a bank loan to buy a house, it's a long-term 30-year loan, where you pay the bank in monthly installments. If you borrow 1,000,000 baht, you'll pay the bank roughly 6,000 baht a month — more or less, depending on the interest rate the bank charges you. Let's assume you get an average rate of 6% per year over 30 years.`,
    `If we run the numbers: 6,000 baht × 12 months equals 72,000 baht, meaning you pay 72,000 baht per year. And 72,000 × 30 years comes to 2,160,000 baht. That means if you borrow 1,000,000 baht, the money you actually pay the bank totals 2,160,000 baht. The extra part is what we call "interest" — it's fair to say you pay more in interest than the principal you borrowed.`,
    `Here's what you need to understand first: each installment you pay the bank is split into two parts. The first is "interest payment," and the second is "principal repayment."`,
    `In the first installment of 6,000 baht, if you owe the bank 1,000,000 baht, your interest burden is 1,000,000 × 6% ÷ 12 months = 5,000 baht. That means of the money you pay the bank each month, 5,000 baht goes to interest and only 1,000 baht repays the principal. In month 2 you pay another 6,000 baht; your interest burden is now 990,000 × 6% ÷ 12 months = 4,950 baht, with 1,050 baht going to the principal.`,
    `As you can see, in the early stage of a home loan, more than 70–80% of each payment goes entirely to interest, so the total debt you owe the bank shrinks very slowly — which in turn keeps your interest burden high.`,
    `So how should we pay off a house to make the debt drop quickly? One method that reduces the interest burden and lets you close out the debt faster is "extra payments" (bpo) — simply put, paying more each month than the regular installment, in this case more than 6,000 baht a month. The extra money you pay goes 100% toward the principal, so your debt drops much faster and you're charged less interest. Simply put, if you pay double the normal amount agreed with the bank, you can pay off the house within 10 years instead of 30 — a full 20 years faster.`,
    `Another method that reduces your interest burden very effectively is "refinancing." These days, when you take out a bank loan there's a promotional "low interest" period for the first 2–3 years. The principle is: once the promo ends and you start getting charged the full rate, go to another bank, take out a new loan, and use it to close the debt at the old bank — so you keep enjoying the low promotional rate indefinitely. Currently, promotional rates average around 3.5%, but once the promo ends the rate jumps to around 5–6%.`,
    `Refinancing is something everyone should do. Just by moving your loan again and again, you cut your interest burden while paying the same monthly amount. If you can keep the promotional rate going continuously, you'll pay off the loan in around 20 years instead of 30. And if you both make 1× extra payments AND keep refinancing, you can pay off your house in just 8–9 years!`,
    `Once you know how to pay off a house fast, the next problem everyone faces is "which bank should I borrow from?" The simple advice: just look at which bank offers the lowest interest rate and go with that one. The best way to find the lowest rate is to diligently walk into several banks and ask, because some banks run great promotions with rates 0.5–1% per year lower than elsewhere. But when comparing rates, look at the "average rate" over the whole period before you can refinance again — not just the promotional rate.`,
    `And if your workplace has a benefit program with a bank for home loans, it's really worth giving that special consideration — you may get a special rate cheaper than borrowing on your own, or easier and faster approval. Always check with your company's HR department first; it's a benefit you're entitled to use as well.`,
  ],

  "ธุรกิจซักรีด — Laundry Business": [
    `Den and Porn run a small laundry shop under an old condo building in the Huai Khwang area. Den used to work in the laundry of a five-star Western hotel; more than ten years of experience made him an expert at laundry — he can use every piece of equipment, knows where to buy supplies cheaply, and knows every kind of cleaning solution. Porn was a housekeeper at the same hotel. The two met — Porn flirted with Den first — and agreed to move in together at Den's house, without any big wedding. Two years after moving in, Porn had their first child and left her job to become a full-time homemaker.`,
    `As the child grew, expenses rose, and Porn discussed with Den that she wanted to help earn income. She began taking in clothes from people in the soi to wash and iron at home, while Den kept working at the hotel. After two years of this, the home laundry work had grown, so Den decided to quit and work together with her. This time they rented an old shophouse on the main road at the mouth of the soi. Business went well, customers grew, and they gained regular customers from two small hostels nearby, because Den knew hotel-style laundering — customers were impressed and started spreading the word.`,
    `Den and Porn saved until they could buy a bigger shophouse than the old one, this time a bit farther from home, about five kilometers. The new shop was beautifully decorated and added dry-cleaning service. Dry cleaning involves lots of detail, so the couple hired helpers — two sisters from Isan. These two are excellent workers: quick learners, good at their jobs, able to help with everything, whether serving customers out front or working in the back.`,
    `When their son got older, he began helping his parents at the shop in the evenings and on holidays. The couple figure that once he finishes his vocational certificate, they'll have him study for a degree in an evening program so he can help at the shop full-time during the day. The son is smart, quick-witted, and hardworking. He often tells his parents he'll help them earn money — if he had a lot of money, he'd expand the shop into a suit and evening gown rental shop, and if the timing is right, maybe even a full wedding shop. Den and Porn dream far ahead to the day they'll be owners of a wedding shop, comfortable at last, no longer struggling as they have for almost thirty years.`,
  ],

  "แม่น้ำโขง — The Mekong River": [
    `The Mekong is an international river flowing through many countries in Asia. It begins as snow in Tibet that melts into small streams that merge together. It then flows through China, where it becomes part of the Yangtze (Chang Jiang) river system, then through Myanmar, where it is called the Salween, then through Laos and Thailand, where it becomes the natural border between Thailand and Laos, known on both sides as the Mekong (or Mae Nam Khong). The junction point of the Mekong between Myanmar, Thailand, and Laos is very famous — it's called the "Golden Triangle."`,
    `The Mekong forms the Thai–Lao border in two stretches. The first is in Chiang Rai province, 180 kilometers long, before it flows into Laos. It becomes the border again at the provinces of Loei, Nong Khai, Bueng Kan, Nakhon Phanom, Mukdahan, Amnat Charoen, and Ubon Ratchathani — a total distance of about 800 kilometers. It then enters Cambodia, where a branch forms the world-famous Tonle Sap lake, then flows into Vietnam and out into the South China Sea. At 4,880 kilometers long, it is the vital artery of Southeast Asia.`,
    `Some compare the Mekong to the Danube in Europe, since it flows through many countries in Asia. What also makes the Mekong fascinating is the great difference in water levels between the rainy and dry seasons. The power of the current in the rainy season turns to drought; small islands appear along the river's course in the dry season, sometimes becoming large areas in or beside the river where people can farm fields and orchards for a whole season.`,
    `In the past, when the water level dropped until dry, people on both banks could easily walk across to visit each other — it was completely normal, because the people of both banks are very close in ancestry, language, religion, beliefs, and culture, especially the belief in the Naga serpent said to be the source of the Mekong. But after the territory was clearly divided into two countries, crossing over became less easy than before. The Thai government, however, has built 4 bridges across the Mekong to the Lao side, with 4 more planned for the future, so crossing back and forth is no longer difficult for the kin on both banks.`,
  ],

  "จดทะเบียนสัตว์เลี้ยง — Pet Registration": [
    `New law update: Bangkok pet registration by 2026 (B.E. 2569). Because so many more pets are now being kept in Bangkok, the BMA has begun regulating pet ownership by issuing rules for keeping animals in the Bangkok area. The key requirement is a cap on the number of pets — especially for popular pets like dogs and cats — limiting the number of animals according to the size of the living space, and requiring pet registration and microchipping. It takes effect January 10, 2026. Owners who fail to comply face legal penalties.`,
    `Documents required to register a pet:`,
    `1. The owner's national ID card`,
    `2. The house registration where the animal lives`,
    `3. A letter of consent from the landlord if you are a "renter"`,
    `4. A certificate`,
    `5. Vaccination certificate (if any)`,
    `6. Certificate of neutering surgery from a veterinarian (if any)`,
    `7. Power of attorney (if any)`,
    `There are also other rules beyond registration. For example: a strong leash must be used, or the animal kept in an appropriate carrier/pen/cage at all times when outside the home; animals must not be allowed to disturb others; and owners must pick up their animal's droppings in public every time. Dangerous dog breeds, or dogs with a history of attacking people, must wear a muzzle, use a strong and secure leash, and be held no more than 50 centimeters from the collar at all times.`,
  ],

  "อาหารเสริมจำเป็นหรือไม่ — Are Supplements Necessary?": [
    `Vitamins and dietary supplements play a bigger role in our daily lives these days — you can tell from the many brands of vitamin and dietary supplements on the market. Do we actually need to take them? And if we take them for a long time, are there harmful effects? Why have people turned to supplements as a way to look after their health?`,
    `We get most vitamins and minerals from vegetables and fruit. Suppose one day we don't get 5 portions of vegetables or fruit — what does "5 portions" mean here? Think of your fist: one fist of vegetables and fruit combined is 1 portion. So if in a day you get fewer than 5 portions of vegetables and fruit, you're not getting enough vitamins and minerals. Then we have to consider whether that person has an underlying condition. If they have a condition that prevents the body from absorbing enough vitamins or minerals, then supplements are worth adding.`,
    `Checking your body's vitamin and mineral levels can be done with a blood test. If we can't get a blood test and we're unsure, but want to buy vitamin supplements to take on our own, doctors advise:`,
    `1. Taking lots of vitamins or supplements doesn't mean it's safe — they can harm the body too, because they can accumulate as residue in the body.`,
    `2. If you want to take vitamins, it's recommended to test your body's vitamin levels first.`,
    `3. Choose water-soluble vitamins, which are safer for the body because they don't accumulate — namely vitamin B and vitamin C.`,
    `4. If you have an underlying condition, consult a doctor, because some vitamins are not recommended. For example, for kidney patients we don't recommend vitamin A supplements, because the vitamin can build up excessively in the liver and harm the body.`,
    `For good health, we don't need to take high doses of vitamin supplements. Instead, focus on eating every kind of food in amounts that are neither too much nor too little, check that the product is properly certified, and remember to take appropriate amounts to reduce accumulation in the body.`,
  ],

  "วัยเกษียณ — Retirement": [
    `Charoen has been a Chonburi native since birth and has never moved away. He studied at a school near home through ninth grade, then went on to a commercial college for three more years. After that he applied for many jobs, until he passed the exam to become a career civil servant at the Bang Lamung district office thirty years ago. He has held many positions; currently he is a senior officer in the registration section, and he is retiring this year at age sixty. He has decided to take his retirement lump sum and invest in land in Buriram province to start a mixed orchard with his husband.`,
    `Charoen's husband is named Sombat. Sombat is from Buriram, three years older than Charoen. They met at a friend's wedding thirty-five years ago. After marrying, Sombat moved into Charoen's home and got a job at the Chonburi electricity authority as a general technician. He retired just a few years ago. The authority had changed its policy for retiring employees, so he received no pension but got a large lump-sum payment instead. Not knowing what to do with it, he put nearly all of it into Government Savings Bank lottery bonds.`,
    `This year Charoen is about to retire, and he'll receive a lump sum as well. In addition, Charoen will get a monthly pension — not much, not too little, enough for the two of them, since the couple has no children. Actually, they had one son, but he became addicted to drugs and died as a teenager. So they've decided to live out their retirement years in Sombat's hometown. The plan is to sell the house in Chonburi and buy ten to fifteen rai of land, build a house, and grow a mixed fruit-and-vegetable orchard, selling to restaurants they know in Pattaya.`,
    `This year Sombat's savings lottery bonds reach their three-year term and can be withdrawn. He has decided to withdraw them and combine them with his spouse's lump sum. All this money should be enough to buy the land. He will hire builders they know well in Chonburi to build the house there, and hire Sombat's relatives — most of whom have generations of orchard experience — to help work the orchard on their land. He thinks that, at the very least, the couple's retirement should be happy and let them do something useful for society, in whatever measure.`,
  ],

  "สถานการณ์ยากลำบาก — A Difficult Situation": [
    `Sompol is worried about the direction of his business over the next few months, because Thailand is now facing the COVID-19 epidemic and it seems to be getting worse, with no sign of easing anytime soon. The government announces new policies every day — some are requests for cooperation, some are orders — and his business has been severely affected.`,
    `Sompol has run a traditional Thai massage shop for eighteen years. His shop is in the basement of an office building in the Khlong Toei area. The shop has moved several times; he moved into the current building only three or four years ago. He now has twelve employees — four men and eight women. Sompol's shop offers many kinds of massage at reasonable prices, and every masseur is government-certified. Most customers are office workers, both regulars and new faces, plus some tourists. It's open every day except Sunday from ten in the morning to eight at night.`,
    `The shop's customers have fallen by more than half since late last year, when the epidemic began in China. Sompol thinks the economy plays a part too — many people are economizing, and massage isn't a life necessity. He understands his customers well. After New Year the shop was quieter than ever; three or four employees asked to leave and return to their home provinces. Not knowing what else to do, he had to let them go. Last month Sompol decided to close on weekends to cut costs, and he kept the shop open even knowing that staying open meant nothing but losses.`,
    `As the epidemic worsened, the Bangkok governor ordered the closure of places where crowds gather, and massage shops had to close. Before closing day, many employees came to say goodbye and return home, saying they might not come back to work at the shop. Many sat hugging one another, holding hands and crying. Sompol stopped himself from crying with them. He turned many plans over in his mind, trying to tell himself that life must find a way. He must get through this hard time. He, his family, and his business must survive. For now, it's just a rest period — one that might be very, very long.`,
  ],

  "ความล้มเหลวของการทำธุรกิจร้านกาแฟ — Coffee Shop Business Failures": [
    `Thailand sees new coffee shop businesses open every month — several a month — with overall market growth of 15%, which is a high figure. But more businesses enter the market than the growth rate can support, so a portion of them get hurt and fall away.`,
    `The main reasons many would-be coffee shop owners can't build something strong, growing, and lasting are mostly these:`,
    `1. Some people think this is a lifestyle business that can be run with a "chill... chill" mindset. But in truth, once you open a coffee shop, it's a real business, with real customers, real expenses, and plenty of chances to fail — because the coffee shop business is hard work that can't be done as a hobby.`,
    `2. Wrong location. Location is the key. You must balance an appropriately sized shop in a good location against rent that isn't too high. Many people choose unsuitable locations — high costs, few customers, isolated. Sometimes a location with many competitors can be good if you're the first one there, but there are downsides: some customers don't want to walk far — they go wherever is closest — or you end up competing for walk-by customers who only want to quench their thirst.`,
    `3. The shop is too small. The formula for shop size says it should seat about 20–30 customers, with customers steadily flowing in and out at no fewer than 2 at a time. If the shop has long stretches without customers, that's a risk of failure — you must quickly find solutions, or better, survey and check before deciding to set up shop.`,
    `4. Lack of standards. Consistent standards are essential. You must maintain standards in everything — product, service, and the shop's atmosphere — so good that the customer feels as though you've married them, being consistent with them in every way. As a customer, they'll give you no more than 2 chances; if you fail to impress them a second time, you'll never have the right to welcome them back for a drink in your coffee shop again.`,
    `5. Weak shop reputation or brand. Most coffee shops serve local people, and owners often neglect this group. Also, some shops want to build a bigger coffee shop but can't create buzz or word-of-mouth, customer-to-customer recommendation. Online marketing done wrong, and lacking a shop story that gives visitors an experience to retell.`,
  ],

  "งาช้าง — Ivory": [
    `A tusk is a tooth, or tooth-like organ, of certain mammals — elephants, walruses, etc. — that extends outside the mouth. It has been highly valuable in the world for thousands of years, because such animals exist only in some parts of the world, creating long-standing demand to find, buy, sell, export, and import ivory worldwide — especially now that producing and trading ivory has become illegal in many countries, with efforts to make that legal effect worldwide.`,
    `Ivory was once an important trade good in Africa and Asia. In Thailand, historical records show it was a commodity whose trade the government controlled, long used as a gift expressing relations between countries. Ivory was used to decorate homes or crafted into objects — bowls, spoons, Buddha images, fans, chairs, boxes, jewelry — for the upper classes of the past.`,
    `The finest ivory in the world is African ivory, and Thailand was long a hub for importing and exporting ivory worldwide. But for Thais who hold supernatural beliefs, Thai ivory commands a higher price, believed to hold special powers. This has led to businesspeople faking ivory — passing off ivory from other countries as Thai ivory, and passing off other materials such as animal bone, animal teeth, stone, and minerals as ivory too.`,
    `When Thailand joined the CITES treaty, laws banned the import, export, production, trade, or ownership of African ivory, as elephants are protected worldwide. But five years ago a law was added banning the production and sale of Thai ivory as well. Those who owned Thai ivory before this law must register with the government as required, and may not own Thai ivory, or objects made from it, exceeding four pieces with a combined weight of no more than half a kilogram.`,
  ],

  "ตู้ขายของอัตโนมัติ — Vending Machines": [
    `Vending machines are a modern sales technology that arose for many reasons. One we have to acknowledge is convenience: this kind of machine works 24 hours a day, takes no holidays or leave, and of course never gets sick — neither genuinely nor "politically" — and never bands together to strike against the employer for higher wages. But that's not the only reason so many businesses have turned to this equipment to replace workers whose conditions grow more demanding by the day.`,
    `Records say the modern vending machine began in England, selling postcards and accepting only coins. Today the technology has advanced so far that you can pay with cash — coins and banknotes — credit cards, online payment systems, and even pick up goods paid for in advance. Products available from vending machines range from drinks, cosmetics, fresh fruit and vegetables, fresh food, and ready-made meals to clothing and more.`,
    `As for Japan, famous for technology, it naturally has more vending machines than any country in the world. The main reason is convenience — umbrella machines outside train stations, boxed noodle machines in front of apartments. The machines also reduce awkwardness between buyer and seller for "private" products such as underwear, condoms, and sanitary pads, and even for "niche" products such as pornographic videos, used women's underwear, and so on.`,
    `However, customers who don't quite accept this way of selling give these reasons: they fear that if the product has a problem, or they don't receive what they paid for, they won't be able to contact anyone, or it may take a long time to get a refund or exchange. Some add that they want to talk with a real person because it feels better — the way they've done all their lives — or they hope to get a small discount from the seller through a chat.`,
  ],

  "วิธีประหยัดไฟ — Saving Electricity": [
    `Electricity bills shooting sky-high! What to do? There's no denying that the one thing that makes life bearable in scorching weather is the air conditioner — the only thing that delivers instant cool. But! The upside has a downside you can't overlook: running the AC all day from morning to evening helps push your electricity bill shockingly high. If you don't want to pay an enormous bill, it's best to run the AC only when necessary — for example while sleeping at night — and use a fan during the day. This hot season, here are my electricity-saving tips to share.`,
    `Use electricity at the right times. Around 10:00 p.m. – 6:00 a.m. is the ideal window for using electricity, because most people are asleep, so appliances run at full efficiency and finish their work faster — which helps cut your bill nicely.`,
    `Unplug appliances every time. When you finish using an appliance, unplug it. Just switching it off still lets current flow through the appliance — which means it's helping send your bill sky-high.`,
    `Set the AC to a sensible temperature. Setting the AC too cold wastes the most power. The right temperature is 25 degrees. Another money-saver: set a timer to switch the AC off about 1 hour before you wake up.`,
    `Choose the right light bulbs. LED bulbs save 90% more electricity than ordinary bulbs, and importantly they last much longer. Don't forget to turn off every light in the house after use.`,
    `Clean the refrigerator. A fridge stuffed too full, or rarely cleaned, is one of the biggest power wasters, because the machine must produce cold constantly. Also, appliances over 3 years old draw more power to operate, driving bills up too — so bid farewell to all the old appliances in your home.`,
    `Use a fan during the day. Use a fan instead of the air conditioner — a fan helps air circulate through the house and consumes far less electricity than AC. Beyond that, your daily habits also help reduce household electricity use.`,
    `Wear light, airy clothing. Wear thin, breathable fabrics, and importantly choose light-colored tops, since they absorb less heat than dark colors. Showering also helps release body heat and leaves you feeling fresh, and applying cooling powder is another way to add coolness to the body.`,
    `Drink cold water. Drinking cold water is another effective way to beat the heat, and drinking plain water often refreshes the body. Finally, opening doors and windows to ventilate is one of the first ways to let breeze into the house, avoid heat buildup, and greatly reduce that stuffy, sweltering feeling.`,
  ],

  "นั่งเครื่องบิน — Taking a Plane": [
    `My partner is someone who is very afraid of airplanes. I've asked many times; he can't really say why he fears them. If we travel together, he'll propose we take the train or drive instead, even though it takes many times longer — he won't change his mind, and he cites a few reasons too.`,
    `First, he fears plane accidents. He tells how, when he was a child, there was a plane crash near his home in Suphan Buri province — an airline from Austria; all the passengers died. The images in the news back then were terrifying.`,
    `Second — not fear, but he claims flying isn't as fast as people think, and it's a hassle. You must check in at least two to three hours before departure and check your luggage first. If you have water or liquids over one hundred milliliters, you can't carry them on board. And flights can be postponed, or problems cause late departures, or you arrive later than scheduled anyway.`,
    `If he truly must fly, he chooses a night flight, when nothing is visible outside the window. Before departure he takes medicine that makes him drowsy and tries to sleep through the whole flight. If he can't sleep, he keeps his eyes shut the entire time.`,
  ],

  "พลาสติก — Plastic": [
    `When I was a child, we didn't use nearly as much plastic as today. When buying food or drinks, we brought our own containers — that was normal, with no one forcing us and no big campaigns like nowadays. Schoolchildren carried lunch boxes, tiffin carriers, and water flasks to school every day. For kids, showing off such things was a point of pride.`,
    `When I went to the fresh market with my mother, she brought a basket. Her basket was woven rattan, beautiful and lightweight. Vegetables, fruit, or meat went straight into the basket. Sometimes vendors would wrap things in banana leaves for us — but we certainly used no plastic at all. Four years ago I traveled to Myanmar, and incredibly, people there still do this. It reminded me of my childhood.`,
    `Today we use much more plastic in Thailand, especially single-use plastic containers. This trend has been around for almost twenty years, starting with clear plastic food bags — both "hot bags" and "cold bags." Hot bags hold food; cold bags hold drinks, tied plump with a rubber band. I once watched clips foreign tourists posted on YouTube — they love this Thai method.`,
    `Not long after came foam food boxes and plastic water bottles. We used plastic for containers more and more, because Thais cook at home less and instead buy ready-made food and drinks outside. Now that many people love iced coffee, iced tea, and fruit smoothies, there are ever more disposable plastic cups. I fear that before long, plastic waste like this will flood the city.`,
  ],

  "ปฏิทิน — Calendars": [
    `Around New Year, many customers receive small gifts. Some gifts are useful; some not so much — once received, they just sit there, with no clear use. But there's one gift customers love and regularly ask for, because it's so useful: the calendar.`,
    `A calendar is used to check the day, month, and year — these days most commonly on the solar system. But calendars in Thailand give dates in several ways, such as Buddhist Era and Christian Era years, plus the lunar calendar system, so months appear both as international months and Thai lunar months. Some people use calendars for work; others use them to check holidays, festivals, or religious observances and beliefs.`,
    `Calendars come in many forms — wall calendars and desk calendars. Thais also used to favor small pocket-diary calendars for jotting things down, but that's no longer necessary since we can view a calendar on our phones or similar devices and add information directly into them.`,
    `Besides dates, calendars carry beautiful pictures — mostly famous people such as actors and singers, or figures Thais revere, such as the King, the Queen, or highly respected monks. Nowadays calendar images are more varied, especially paintings by famous artists that companies like to buy, keep, and print in their calendars.`,
  ],

  "งานศพ — Funerals": [
    `When someone dies, relatives must promptly notify a government official as required by law — either a local official or one at the district or provincial level. Then comes preparing the funeral, a major event for the family. Many people must ask for several days off work; many company rules grant up to seven days' leave for a parent, spouse, or child. Sometimes people use vacation days instead. In practice, Thais are quite accepting of taking leave when a family member dies.`,
    `Most Thais are Buddhist, so the family must contact a temple to book a pavilion (sala) for the funeral. Families with enough space at home may hold the funeral there, but the temple is certainly more convenient in many cases. Within the first twenty-four hours, a bathing ceremony may be held: the body is laid out with one hand extended so guests can pour water over it, to ask forgiveness for past wrongs or wish the departed a peaceful passing. For this ceremony, the family invites only a few truly close guests.`,
    `Then the body is placed in a coffin and set in the pavilion, decorated with small tables, colored lights, and masses of flowers. Beside the coffin stands a large portrait of the deceased. The family keeps the body for rites like this for about three to seven days; if the deceased was an important person, ceremonies may run fifty or even a hundred days. Daytime activities are light — monks may be invited to chant in late morning, followed by a meal offering. In the evening many guests are invited, with monks chanting from about 19:00–20:00, after which the host serves guests a light meal.`,
    `The truly big event is the cremation, held in the afternoon after the final temple day. If the body was kept at home, it must be moved to the temple for cremation, since most crematoria are at temples. The cremation draws more guests than any other day. The host family prepares flowers made of fragrant wood or scented white paper for guests to lay in respect at the coffin. Hosts may give guests small keepsakes too — books, drinking glasses, and so on. After the guests leave, the actual cremation begins, taking no more than three hours. The next day the family collects the bones to keep at home or at the temple, or sometimes scatters them on the sea or a river.`,
  ],

  "ครอบครัวใหญ่ — Big Families": [
    `Thai families have many members, with several generations living together — grandparents, parents, uncles and aunts, siblings, and grandchildren. Everyone seems so close. A single family might have almost ten members. That was very strange to me, because I wondered: with that many people, how do they live together in a small house?`,
    `I come from a small family in Gold Coast, Australia. I grew up with my mother and younger sister. Dad split up with Mom before my sister was born — I must have been five or six. My mother is Australian; my father was German. When he left, he went back to his country, but my sister and I still saw him regularly; we visited every school break.`,
    `In my father's family there were my grandfather, grandmother, and my youngest aunt. She was single — once married, but long divorced. She had no children, so she moved back to my grandparents' house. I felt my father's family was quite large, though nothing like a Thai family. I wasn't very close to them. Sometimes I felt suffocated staying in a house with so many people. Worst of all, when staying with Dad I had to share a room with my sister — I remember hating it; we fought almost daily. But after Dad died when I was twenty-three, I never went back to Germany.`,
    `On holidays, my Thai friends like to ask whether I'll go home to see my mother and sister in Australia. I answer that I don't want to go back often — maybe every two years. If I have a lot of time, I'd rather travel abroad alone. Thais surely think that's very strange.`,
  ],

  "โชคดีโชคร้าย — Luck & Beliefs": [
    `Thais hold many beliefs. Some come from religion, especially Buddhism — this life, the next life, rebirth after death depending on the karma made in this life. But some beliefs have no clear origin; they're beliefs passed down by earlier generations. Some people believe them, some don't, and some half-believe.`,
    `Beliefs link what you do now to influence over your future. For example: cut your hair on Wednesday and life will bring trouble; wear the color of the day and you'll have good luck. Whole businesses have sprung up in Thailand because of beliefs — license plates ending in nine, or with many nines, fetch high prices; you can't get them free, you must bid for them from the government. Visit a temple with a sacred shrine and you'll find shops selling flowers, incense, candles, sweets, and colored cloth that people buy as offerings.`,
    `Some beliefs are ancient, but others are new — lucky phone numbers, for instance, a craze that arose in Thailand within the last fifteen years or so. Likewise the belief in lucky jewelry and charms, sold at high prices despite being made from materials that cost very little.`,
    `Thais have also adopted beliefs from abroad: many buildings have no thirteenth floor, following Western belief, and homes are designed and arranged by Chinese feng shui. The funny thing is that some beliefs — no one is sure where they came from, yet people mention them often. For example: eat the last piece of food on the plate and you'll end up with a beautiful or handsome partner.`,
  ],

  "อาหารตามสั่ง — Street Food": [
    `Everyone complains they have no time — no time to rest, no time to travel, no time to cook. Yet strangely, everyone has time to eat. When you don't cook, you have to buy food outside. Many people dislike ready-made packaged food, feeling it isn't fresh — they prefer food cooked hot and fresh to order.`,
    `Made-to-order food, or one-plate dishes, has thus become an appealing option for people with no time, because you can find a made-to-order food shop everywhere — roadside, in malls, in markets; morning, noon, evening, even late at night. The strong points of these shops: lots of menu choices, quick cooking, and low prices.`,
    `The most popular menus are rice or noodles with a variety of meats and vegetables that aren't too unusual. The problem is that made-to-order food can carry more calories than the body needs; the cooking uses no small amount of oil — stir-frying and deep-frying — which leads to obesity, and the vegetables included are few in quantity and variety. Eat it often and you can run short of essential vitamins.`,
  ],

  "ทหารไทย — Thai Military": [
    `In Thailand we rarely see female soldiers, because the Thai armed forces don't much encourage women to join. It starts early: boys wanting to be soldiers can apply to cadet and NCO academies at ages 16–18, and upon graduating become soldiers immediately. For women, the military opens recruitment exams only when positions fall vacant, and such positions are few — mostly document work, accounting, languages, teaching, or other support roles.`,
    `Currently there are about 360,000 career positions in the armed forces: 240,000 army, 70,000 navy, 50,000 air force. There is no clear data on female soldiers within this number, but it's plain they are far fewer. In addition, about 80,000–100,000 conscripts enter the forces each year for 1–2 years of service.`,
    `By law, conscripts must be male only. Every Thai man holding Thai citizenship from birth must register for military service at age 18, and conscription occurs once a year in April when they turn 21. Men in good health, without chronic disease or disability and without other grounds for exemption, must join the draft. The number of conscripts the army wants each year differs by province and can change. Also, men aged 21 can volunteer as conscripts without waiting for the draft.`,
    `If the number of volunteers equals or exceeds what the army wants, no selection is needed. The selection is called "drawing black and red cards": 21-year-old men who meet army standards draw lots — a black card means no conscription; a red card means you serve. Service periods differ too: a bachelor's degree cuts service to just 1 year, and volunteering also means 1 year. Afterward, one can apply to become a career soldier in the armed forces.`,
  ],

  "ซอยตัน — Dead-End Alleys": [
    `Soi Lat Phrao 82/1 is an old, narrow soi that hardly anyone knows. When you hail a taxi there, drivers tend to turn wrongly into Soi 82, which comes first — perhaps because it's a wide soi with two convenience stores and a major bank branch at its mouth, plus a motorcycle-taxi stand visible from afar. If you tell them too late, the taxi has to waste time making a U-turn mid-soi and coming back out, because the two sois don't connect.`,
    `At the mouth of Soi 82/1 stand old shophouses — a small grocery and a dim Chinese pharmacy — and a big tree blocks the entrance, hiding the fact that it's a soi at all. The soi is so narrow cars can't pass each other; before driving in, you must check carefully whether a car is coming out, and wait if so — otherwise it's chaos and wasted time. Besides the narrow road, residents along the way like to put out plants and ornaments that eat into the roadway, making it even narrower. Speeding motorbikes often crash into these things, but the owners keep doing it anyway.`,
    `Besides being very narrow, it's also a dead-end soi. From the entrance to the dead end is about five hundred meters. At the end stands a big old wooden house, near collapse, probably over eighty years old — unclear whether anyone lives there or it's abandoned. The door is shut tight with a big padlock outside. Old-timers in the soi say the government once asked to cut a road through the property to connect to another soi, but the landowner refused. Someone once offered to buy the house for a restaurant — no sale. Anyone who drives up to this house and wants to leave must reverse all the way out, since there's nowhere to turn around — hence the many signs posted throughout the soi warning it's a dead end.`,
    `Recently, construction began on an electric rail line running along Lat Phrao Road, with one station in front of Soi Lat Phrao 84. Buyers have come asking for land in the neighboring sois to build condos, small malls, and hostels. Residents of Soi 82/1 have started selling their houses, from the entrance to mid-soi. Before long, this soi will change greatly — cars coming and going constantly. I don't want to imagine how much more chaotic it will be with more cars while the soi stays as small and narrow as ever.`,
  ],

  "เวลามาตรฐาน — Standard Time": [
    `Before clocks, Thais told time by the sun. This method may not be very precise, but it could tell you it was morning, late morning, noon, afternoon, or evening — because Thailand is a tropical country with sunshine all year, and sunrise and sunset times differ little between seasons, no more than forty minutes. Just seeing the sunlight and feeling the changing temperature was enough to tell the time.`,
    `In the past, Thais used other natural signs to tell time too: rooster crows in the morning, birdsong on branches in the evening, tree shadows shifting on the ground through the day, the wind, the direction of sunlight. But this took very careful observation, since each season differs.`,
    `Today we use clocks, which tell time precisely in hours and minutes. Thailand's first standard time was six hours forty minutes ahead of Greenwich, England. But after nations met at Greenwich in B.E. 2462 (1919), Thailand decided to change its national standard time to seven hours ahead of Greenwich Mean Time.`,
    `This new standard uses the time at Thailand's easternmost point, not Bangkok time (an eighteen-minute difference), and there is no policy of seasonal clock changes when sunrise and sunset shift, as in many countries in Europe and America.`,
  ],

  "ผัดไทย — Pad Thai": [
    `Pad Thai is one of Thailand's most famous dishes, well known not only at home but popular around the world. Walk into any Thai restaurant abroad and you can be sure Pad Thai is on the menu as an indispensable crowd-drawer. Many foreigners know it and have tasted it many times — but the Pad Thai different people know, have seen, and have tasted may well not share the same ingredients, appearance, or flavor.`,
    `Pad Thai is not an ancient Thai dish. Starting from its main ingredient alone, it can hardly be called Thai: the dish uses thin rice noodles that certainly come from Chinese culture, adapted with ingredients and seasonings to suit Thai tastes, until it no longer felt like foreign food. Finally the word "Thai" was appended to declare this dish Thai, not Chinese.`,
    `Pad Thai developed from a traditional dish long present in Thailand: stir-fried noodles (kuai-tiao phat). That dish has recipes varying by locality. The main ingredients are rice noodles, various sauces, fish sauce, sugar, chili, lime, and meats such as pork, shrimp, or chicken. Vegetables vary widely, the most popular being bean sprouts, spring onion or Chinese chives, sometimes with an egg added.`,
    `Pad Thai arose during World War II, when food was expensive and scarce. The government of the time wanted people to switch to noodles, which were cheaper and more filling. It was also a chance to create jobs for Thais, because this is a one-plate dish rarely cooked at home — people eat it at cheap roadside food shops. "Pad Thai" comes from the full name kuai-tiao phat Thai — "Thai-style stir-fried noodles" — for its Thai ingredients like tamarind paste, lime, and chili. Later a government promoting nationalist policy shortened the name to sound more Thai, leaving just "Pad Thai."`,
  ],

  "สูบบุหรี่ — Smoking": [
    `Per recent government statistics, 17.4 percent of Thais aged fifteen and over smoke — nearly ten million people: 34.7 percent of men and 1.3 percent of women, divided into regular smokers at 15.3 percent and occasional smokers at 2.1 percent. That said, the number of Thai smokers keeps falling compared with B.E. 2534 (1991), when as much as thirty-two percent of the entire Thai population smoked.`,
    `Thai law controls tobacco strictly. For example: no sales to youth under twenty, with ID checks required at every purchase; no one under eighteen may sell cigarettes; cigarettes may not be openly displayed, though a sign saying cigarettes are sold is allowed; no selling single cigarettes — only whole packs; no advertising of these products; and no smoking in public places such as restaurants, parks, restrooms, streets, and schools.`,
    `Even as the law tries to control tobacco and steadily shrink the number of smokers by curious methods — printing images of smoking's harms on packs, and censoring images of smoking on television — the government itself produces cigarettes, holding a legal monopoly on domestic production, which makes imported cigarettes far more expensive through import taxes that rise almost yearly.`,
    `Many now disagree with the law banning the import of e-cigarettes, unsure what the government's reasoning is. Yet even though they're illegal, we still see people buying and using e-cigarettes in Thailand without being arrested. More striking, the number of vapers keeps growing alarmingly: government statistics show e-cigarette users rose from 1,700 in B.E. 2560 (2017) to 78,740 in B.E. 2564 (2021) — an increase of 4,500 percent in just 4 years.`,
  ],

  "บัตรเอทีเอ็ม — ATM Cards": [
    `Today there are many ways to withdraw money from the bank; we no longer need to withdraw in person at a branch as before. But the method everyone knows and still uses is withdrawing with an ATM card. If you have a bank account (savings accounts only), you can request an ATM card, though fees vary by bank.`,
    `The first-time card fee starts at two hundred baht, but can cost more if bundled with other services such as life insurance or account-alert services, and there's an annual fee too. Some banks offer interesting extras, such as your photo on the card, or images of popular celebrities or cartoons.`,
    `Normally we withdraw with the card at an ATM, and ATMs are everywhere. Withdrawing at your own bank's machine within the same region carries no fee. But withdrawing at another bank's machine more than three times a month costs ten baht per withdrawal from the fourth time on, and withdrawing outside your account's region costs fifteen to twenty-five baht every time. Using the card abroad costs even more.`,
    `You must enter your PIN each time you withdraw. The PIN has six digits, all numbers. Enter it wrong more than three times and the machine swallows the card; the card becomes unusable and you must get a new one at the bank. Also, if you choose an ATM card that doubles as a debit card, you can swipe it for purchases like a credit card.`,
  ],

  "ปั๊มน้ำมัน — Petrol Stations": [
    `Petrol stations in Thailand look alike — recognizable from far away. Most have a big, tall sign showing the company name and logo, sometimes with signs for the shops inside the station too — coffee shops, convenience stores, restaurants, and so on. Some stations are as big as shopping centers, especially those along major intercity roads.`,
    `Beyond fuel, stations offer other services, many free: restrooms, shower rooms, rest areas, tire-inflation points, ATMs. And varied businesses — restaurants, coffee shops, repair garages, car washes, souvenir shops — are scattered around. Long-distance travelers like to take long breaks at big stations; during festivals you might find nearly fifty tour buses parked at once. Stations around Bangkok also serve as small shopping centers.`,
    `At the pump, we don't fill up ourselves — attendants, fondly called "pump kids," stand ready to serve. There are several fuel types: benzine, diesel, gasohol. You can pay cash or credit card. Stations sometimes run attractive promotions, like free drinking water when you fill up over eight hundred baht. There are also member cards collecting points redeemable for goods, and some credit cards give small fuel discounts too.`,
    `Fuel prices in Thailand shift often with world oil prices. Right now fuel isn't too expensive, averaging thirty-two baht per liter. During the big price spikes of the past decade or so, it once hit forty-three baht per liter. Also, in provinces far from Bangkok fuel costs slightly more due to transport costs. When economic and political situations require, the government may announce measures such as ordering stations closed at night, even though they normally open twenty-four hours.`,
  ],

  "หมอกควันพิษ — Toxic Smog": [
    `Over recent decades, air pollution has become something everyone talks about. The problem is most visible in the North, where smog blankets whole cities in the dry season, causing health problems — stinging eyes, burning noses, difficulty breathing. People with allergies may get rashes or persistent itching for long periods.`,
    `Locals in the North know well that the problem comes from burning agricultural land — rice and corn fields — after harvest to prepare the soil for the next planting. Farmers in northern Thailand and neighboring countries favor this method because it's easy and cheapest. Beyond that, it comes from burning forest to forage, or to help certain expensive mushrooms grow in the forest for collection and sale.`,
    `It's not that no burning happens in other seasons, but in the dry season there's no wind to carry the smog away quickly, and hardly any rain to knock it down. So it seems air pollution lasts only four or five months a year — in truth it's constant; only the severity differs.`,
    `In the past three or four years, the toxic smog problem has reached other regions of Thailand too, as agricultural burning increases. With world food demand rising fast, crop prices climb, and everyone wants to invest in agribusiness. Pollution also comes from other sources: old engines belching black smoke — especially diesel engines — plus the never-ending construction in big cities.`,
  ],

  "สัตว์รบกวน — Pests": [
    `Thailand lies in the world's tropical zone — heavy rain, hot and humid weather, abundant plant and animal life, plenty of natural food — so it has diverse wildlife. Some animals live in the forest and avoid people, but some like living in cities where people are densely settled.`,
    `Some animals are venomous and dangerous; others are harmless but still a nuisance, dirtying the area or provoking disgust. Among the most feared are venomous animals, which can sometimes hurt people when startled or scared, though mostly they don't strike first — snakes, spiders, centipedes.`,
    `Snakes like dry, warm places. During floods they flee up trees or into houses — especially ceilings, where their food, like rats, is plentiful. Spiders and centipedes prefer quiet spots with few people; walk close to an abandoned house or a garbage pile and you'll see them emerge.`,
    `Another group, not very venomous but disruptive to people's peace, is insects such as mosquitoes and flies. Both multiply in hot weather and decline when it cools. Normally a mosquito just bites, nothing worse; but for people allergic to mosquitoes, bites can swell, itch, or become sores if scratched hard. Moreover, both mosquitoes and flies carry pathogens that can infect us. When they multiply around the house, you must find ways to destroy them — spraying insecticide, or having a specialist company come spray every three months.`,
  ],

  "ห้องเช่า — Rental Rooms": [
    `Many Thais must move to big cities to find work, which means moving to live there too. Without family or relatives there, you'd have to buy a house or condo — but many can't afford that, so they live in rental rooms.`,
    `Finding a rental room isn't hard; ads are everywhere. The price depends on the neighborhood, the room's size, whether it has air conditioning, whether it's furnished, and whether there's an elevator.`,
    `Before renting, you pay a deposit — sometimes one month, sometimes more. You get the deposit back when you stop renting, though perhaps not all of it, since the landlord deducts for damage. There's also one month's advance rent. All told, to start renting you need about two to three months' rent in hand.`,
    `Renting requires a contract, though many people don't bother. The contract spells out many things — how water and electricity are charged, by meter or by unit, and how many months' notice you must give before moving out. Read it carefully; otherwise there will surely be disputes.`,
  ],

  "หวยใต้ดิน — Underground Lottery": [
    `Besides the government lottery, Thais love playing the underground lottery too — even though it's illegal gambling, Thais adore it. Many buy underground numbers alongside official lottery tickets every draw when they get a good number. This gray business reaches Thais of every group and every level, despite having no storefront and no certifying documents.`,
    `Who owns the underground lottery business? Nobody knows. We call the owner the "lottery banker" (jao mue huai). No one knows exactly how many bankers there are in Thailand, but underground lottery fans know their "number runners" or "number takers" better, since these are mostly close acquaintances or people they know well — maybe a coworker, a neighbor, or a vendor who sells other goods on the side.`,
    `The underground lottery uses winning numbers from the government lottery — last two digits, last three digits, first three digits — so there are two draws a month, at the start and middle of the month. You can pick any numbers you like, and permutate them too. Payouts come at various rates, such as seventy baht per baht, sixty-five per baht, or five hundred fifty per baht. If you win, you collect straight from the number runner.`,
    `Governments have repeatedly tried to make the underground lottery disappear from Thailand, since it's illegal gambling: the revenue doesn't reach the state, no taxes are paid, and it spawns other problems — murder cases, loan-sharking. One government even tried launching an "above-ground lottery" to compete, but it didn't last long before being scrapped.`,
  ],

  "รถไฟไทย — Thai Railways": [
    `Thai railways are a state enterprise, in service for over a hundred years. The first routes opened were short, from Bangkok to Nakhon Ratchasima. Today there are four main lines: the Northern, Northeastern (Isan), Southern, and Eastern lines.`,
    `Thai rail has two main stations in Bangkok: Hua Lamphong station, from which you can travel every route in the country, and Thonburi station. Recently Thailand opened a new central station in Bangkok — Bang Sue Grand Station, the largest railway station in Southeast Asia.`,
    `Many Thais like taking the train because it's cheap, but many choose rail travel for the old-world atmosphere of the stations and the mostly natural scenery along the way.`,
  ],

  "น้ำดื่ม — Drinking Water": [
    `Our bodies are more than seventy percent water. Water is a key component of blood, saliva, tears, and more — we simply cannot go without it. A human might survive without food for three or four months, but without any water, death can come within a week.`,
    `The advice is to drink at least two liters of water a day, and it should be pure plain water — so tea, coffee, milk, and juice don't count toward the two liters. That said, we also get water from the food in every meal: water is a component of vegetables, fruit, milk, honey, and so on — even meat and beans contain water. If you can't drink water, or none is available, you can eat water-rich foods instead, such as vegetables, fruit, and milk.`,
    `Because water matters so much to the body, many people try to drink the cleanest water they can. Many Thais avoid drinking tap water straight from the faucet; if they must, they boil or filter it first — which is why the water-filter business thrives in Thailand. Others buy drinking water in bottles or plastic tanks, available everywhere, and bulk purchases even come with free home delivery.`,
    `Beyond cleanliness, temperature matters too. The advice is not to drink cold water but rather room-temperature water, especially when feeling unwell or having a sore throat. Warm water seems the healthier choice — and a small squeeze of lime into warm water helps even more.`,
  ],

  "โรงจำนำ — Pawnshops": [
    `When money troubles strike, the pawnshop is a place many people think of, because pawnshops are a very convenient lending business: no need for lots of documents, little time required, and you get cash to use right away.`,
    `When you need money, you can bring valuables — diamonds, gold, watches, mobile phones, televisions — to the pawnshop. The staff inspect the item and appraise it. The contract term is no more than three months; if you don't bring the money back, the pawnshop keeps the item.`,
    `The pawnshop is a hero to many — an option when no one else can help. Best of all, nobody knows you're having money problems, which saves face.`,
  ],

  "ค่าไฟฟ้า — Electricity Bills": [
    `Every month an electricity authority officer walks down the soi checking the electric meters in front of the houses. He points a small handheld device at the meter and prints out the electricity bill.`,
    `In Bangkok the bill comes mid-month. The calculation is complicated because it uses a progressive rate: the more electricity you use, the more you pay — two or three times as much.`,
    `For those in rental rooms, landlords charge more for electricity than the utility does. For utility bills, you can pay at an electricity authority office, 7-Eleven, supermarkets, ATMs, or mobile banking apps — but you must pay before the 5th of each month, or your power gets cut.`,
  ],

  "ขายของออนไลน์ — Online Selling": [
    `Over the past decade, many merchants have switched to selling on the internet, for many reasons. Some say there's no shop rent to pay and no staff to hire — you can do it all alone. Others say it's convenient: you can sell online while holding down a regular job.`,
    `I myself became an online seller in my second year of university. I knew several friends who traveled abroad often, so I asked them to buy affordable brand-name cosmetics for me — from Korea, Japan, Hong Kong.`,
    `Later I began using LINE to contact customers and posting photos on Instagram, opening more channels to reach customers than before. Sometimes customers lack confidence and don't dare transfer money before the goods ship — they're afraid of being cheated.`,
  ],

  "แรงงานต่างด้าว — Migrant Workers": [
    `For the past twenty years, Thailand has opened up to workers from neighboring countries — Laos, Cambodia, Myanmar, Vietnam — to work in many occupations, both legally and illegally. Some provinces have more migrant workers than Thais.`,
    `The reason Thailand takes in more of these workers is a labor shortage, especially for lower-wage work. You'll see many migrant workers in seafood factories or plastics factories.`,
    `The government admits this is a national problem: large numbers of foreigners enter the country illegally. These workers are unregistered, pay no taxes, and have no social security or health insurance.`,
  ],

  "การลงทุน — Investing": [
    `I've been a bank employee for six years. The salary is neither great nor terrible, but given today's economy, I think a regular job alone isn't enough — expenses keep rising, and I'd like more money to spend as I please.`,
    `Having worked at a bank so long, I know how to make money grow. Thirty years ago, my parents' generation only liked depositing money in the bank, because back then deposit interest was very high — nine to eleven percent a year. In those days everyone liked to say that with big deposits you could live comfortably off the interest without working at all.`,
    `Today deposit interest has fallen sharply, to under one percent, so I decided not to keep money in the bank. A friend suggested playing the stock market. I tried it for two years and made good money — genuinely interesting — but nothing is certain, and I feared losses, so I had to find another way.`,
    `Now I've switched to gold, trading it online, because gold prices don't swing as fast as stocks. I watch the price daily: when it rises I sell; when it falls I buy again. I think I have a fair amount of experience now, though I'm still not that skilled. Even so, I'm already making over twenty percent profit a year.`,
    `In the future, if I have more money, I may invest in opening a small shop — my partner minding it by day, and I helping in the evening after work. Many people say I'm dreaming beyond my means. I agree. But working at the bank my whole life? Definitely not for me.`,
  ],

  "สิทธิผู้พิการ — Disability Rights": [
    `People with disabilities are those with physical or mental impairments of various kinds. Disability creates different challenges in daily life — being unable to see, speak, walk, care for oneself, or perhaps move at all. Disabled people have some access to aids that improve quality of life, such as hearing aids, Braille, elevators in public places, and prosthetic limbs — but some disabilities are hard to assist, such as brain-related disabilities.`,
    `Disabled people deserve rights equal to everyone else in daily life, plus policies of special assistance. The government has a disability registration program granting free hospital treatment and a monthly allowance of eight hundred baht — but that hardly seems enough. Many disabled people don't want money from the government; they want more ease of living — wheelchair ramps, voice output devices for the deaf, and so on — that would let them live closer to the lives of the able-bodied.`,
    `Crucially, many disabled people never get the chance to study, for lack of learning equipment suited to them. Furthermore, after graduating they can't find work, because companies try to avoid hiring disabled workers, and Thai law doesn't compel them to. So many disabled people must earn income on their own — mostly selling lottery tickets, since the government reserves a lottery quota specifically for disabled sellers. Some walk the streets selling small items, or may have to beg.`,
    `Disabled people in Thailand have long fought for equal rights. Not long ago, a group of disabled people in Bangkok jointly sued in court to force the BTS skytrain company to build elevators at every station. In the end the court ruled for the disabled group, and BTS built elevators at every station. This may be the first case showing that disabled people are beginning to gain more rights in society — and we may yet see better things ahead.`,
  ],

  "เปิดท้ายขายของ — Car Boot Sales": [
    `In hard economic times, many people try to earn income from every direction. Even if some work pays less than before, it's still better than nothing — the thinking is that earning each day's food money is enough. But with no work at all, you must find a way out and survive first. With few choices left, you dig through what you have at home to sell. Some things fetch good money, some very little, but it beats keeping junk around for nothing.`,
    `In 1998 (B.E. 2541), a major economic crisis hit Thailand. Many businesses went bankrupt; those that didn't close had to cut staff or salaries. People who lost jobs began seeking income to cover small expenses, with profits just enough for utility bills, without much investment. This mindset gave rise to the first car boot markets — markets using empty spaces never used as markets before, like parking lots or football fields, with no rent or very little.`,
    `Sellers at these markets invest very little. Many who own a pickup truck simply park at the car boot market, open the tailgate, lay out their goods at the back, and sit selling beside the truck — easy, cheap. When done selling, they close the tailgate and head straight home. Fast, convenient, economical. The goods vary widely: electric appliances, landline phones, empty photo albums, musical instruments, movie and music CDs. Most are secondhand, but with luck you might find old-stock new items cheaper than the regular market.`,
    `Items for sale may be personal things the owner has only one of — no choice of size, color, or model — so customers take their time choosing. This was the first spark of the secondhand fashion craze that later took off. Some brand-name shoes, bags, and clothes at car boot markets are imported goods discounted by more than half. But know this: there's no warranty — if you're not satisfied, you can't return or exchange. These car boot markets have evolved into today's secondhand and vintage shops.`,
  ],
};
